Answer: **theia** is a technique for localizing structural bugs in deep learning programs—such as issues with activation functions, layer/model properties, loss functions, data preprocessing, or missing/redundant/wrong layers—and suggesting fixes. it uses dataset characteristics and parsed model source code, builds a library-independent meta-model, and applies context-sensitive analyses with verification rules. it supports fcnns and cnns for regression and classification, works with keras and pytorch, and detects bugs at the start of training with location and fix recommendations. it found 57/75 bugs in 40 real-world buggy dl programs.

**neuralint** is a prior/state-of-the-art tool used as a comparison point. it detected 17/75 bugs overall—13/45 in multi-class classification programs and 4/30 in binary classification and regression programs—making it less effective than theia in the reported evaluation.
